\begin{abstract}
Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) avoid collapse through design heuristics, and existing theory bounds the rank they keep but does not predict it.
In a linear JEPA with weight decay the collapsed point is stable, so a direction of the representation survives only by winning a race: it must grow large enough to take over the predictor before the predictor, shrunk by weight decay, leaves the range in which that direction can grow.
This criterion has no fitted constants.
On \EoneBAlignedRuns{} held-out runs with aligned statistics, registered in advance, it predicts the rank with a mean error of \EoneBAlignedMae{} directions, against \EoneBAlignedWindowMae{} for counting the directions that grow at initialisation and \EoneBAlignedBoundMae{} for the static fixed-point threshold.
Registered tests mark its reach in linear models: modified forms predict the count under AdamW at a fixed horizon (not at convergence), with a layer-normalised target, and in deep linear encoders with aligned factors, while a model of weight-decay schedules fails.
The race assumes that collapse is decided early; in an I-JEPA-style vision transformer this premise fails, since raising weight decay at epoch 20 of 50 still removes directions.
In the linear models we test, under constant weight decay, collapse is a race against the predictor's decay; where it is not decided early, a criterion read early in training must take the remaining schedule into account.
\end{abstract}
